- What metrics would tie cost to business value beyond raw savings?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design a practical on-device data structure with incremental updates, offline-first UX, and a clear 12‑month cost plan, including privacy considerations.

## Key Concepts

- Local inverted index and incremental updates
- Offline-first data paths and cache invalidation
- Delta-based cloud sync and conflict resolution
- Resource budgeting: storage, CPU, memory, latency, energy
- Privacy, compliance, and user consent handling

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal incremental inverted index (conceptual)
class InvertedIndex {
  constructor() {
    this.index = {}; // term -> Set of note IDs
  }
  addNote(id, text) {
    text.split(/\W+/).forEach(t => {
      if (!t) return;
      const w = t.toLowerCase();
      (this.index[w] ||= new Set()).add(id);
    });
  }
  search(q) {
    return q
      .toLowerCase()
      .split(/\s+/)
      .flatMap(w => Array.from(this.index[w] || []));
  }
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you estimate index maintenance energy and storage growth over time?
- How would you handle search latency guarantees on mid-range devices with thousands of notes?
- What privacy safeguards would you implement for on-device indexing and sync deltas?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to scope CBAs for privacy-preserving on-device ML and quantify energy, storage, bandwidth, and compliance trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Federated learning
- Gradient compression
- Privacy/compliance overhead
- Cost modeling
- Rollout strategy

## Code Example
```javascript
function computeCBA(params){
  const {devices, energyPerDevice, uplink, cloud, retention} = params;
  const energy = devices * energyPerDevice;
  const total = energy + uplink + cloud + retention;
  const breakeven = total / Math.max(1, (params.revenuePerUser || 1));
  return {energy, total, breakeven};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle drift and model staleness in rollout?
- What metrics would you track to decide go/no-go, and how would you test them in a pilot?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a privacy-conscious, low-latency analytics pipeline under multi-region constraints, with DP guarantees and drift detection.

## Key Concepts
- Real-time streaming with Kafka + Flink
- Differential privacy (epsilon ~1.0)
- Region-local state and windowed aggregations (<100 ms)
- Drift detection and observability
- Data residency and governance

## Code Example
```javascript
function addDPNoise(count, epsilon) {
  const sigma = Math.sqrt(2 / epsilon);
  const noise = randn_bm() * sigma;
  return Math.max(0, Math.round(count + noise));
}
function randn_bm() {
  let u = 0, v = 0;
  while (u === 0) u = Math.random();
  while (v === 0) v = Math.random();
  return Math.sqrt(-2.0 * Math.log(u)) * Math.cos(2 * Math.PI * v);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate privacy guarantees in production?
- What data contracts would you enforce with product teams to prevent over-collection?
